---
import ProjectPost from "../../../components/ProjectPost.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import cover from "./20240127-4.png";
import img3 from "./20240127-3.png";
import img5 from "./20240127-5.png";
import img6 from "./20240127-6.png";
import img7 from "./20240127-7.png";
import img8 from "./20240127-8.png";

export const meta = {
    title: "Splitting one Angular app into three",
    date: new Date(2024, 0, 27),
    description:
        "Three products, one Angular codebase, and a lot of if-elses to untangle",
    tech: ["angular"],
    role: ["Engineering"],
    image: cover,
};
---

<ProjectPost meta={meta}>
    <p>
        <strong>Why rewrite?</strong>
        <a href="https://cryptlex.com">Cryptlex</a> is software licensing service
        that independent software vendors (ISV) embed into their software to enforce
        entitlements in their software. Cryptlex's web applications primarily serves
        three audiences: Managers and developers at the ISV, Resellers of the software,
        Customers who have bought software from the ISV.
    </p><p>
        The above users have diverse and disjoint needs. The existing Angular
        SPA had a lot of botched if-elses to accomodate the above mentioned
        users. However, as we started shipping new features more frequently it
        became difficult to maintain the application for all three user personas
        when the feature could be affecting just one persona. The existing
        application also contained a lot of technical debt. We decided to
        rewrite the application in phases and split the application into three
        applications, the <code>admin-portal</code>, <code>reseller-portal</code
        >, and <code>customer-portal</code>.
    </p><p>
        <strong>Why Angular?</strong> The CTO and the frontend engineers have been
        working with Angular since the start and wanted to continue using the framework.
        I made the decision to stick to prototyping directly in code <a
            href="https://world.hey.com/dhh/design-for-the-web-without-figma-4bc3a218"
            >instead of creating an abstraction in Figma</a
        > due to the small size of the team. However, I was still quite keen on finding
        effective ways to document design decisions.
    </p><p>
        So it was established that we will build three Angular applications, and
        a component library that is shared across the three. We decided to base
        the user interfaces on the Material Design spec since I was fairly
        familiar with all iterations of the <a href="https://m3.material.io/"
            >specification</a
        > and consider it to be mature specification for building mobile-first applications.
    </p><p>Using Angular comes with a lot of woes, let's talk about them.</p><h3
    >
        Angular Material follows the old Material design spec
    </h3><p>
        Despite Google's design team parting ways with the <a
            href="https://mediu<h2>Angular? WHY?</h2>m.com/google-design/the-evolution-of-material-designs-text-fields-603688b3fe03"
            >underline input field</a
        >, <a
            href="https://m3.material.io/components/buttons/overview#388f6096-c34d-4a22-8b1f-11ad69963f7c"
            >all caps text in buttons</a
        >, and many other design flaws in the design specification, a large
        portion of the library <a
            href="https://github.com/angular/components/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3A%22material+spec%22"
            >has not evolved past these issues</a
        >. This forced me to start with creating a custom button, tabs, and
        stepper component.
    </p><div class="post-two-col">
        <figure class="mb-8">
            <Image
                src={img3}
                alt="Angular Material using an earlier implementation of the Tabs in the Material Design specification"
            />
            <figcaption class="mt-2 mb-4 max-w-prose font-medium opacity-80">
                The default tabs have translucent buttons on the tabs with
                inadequate affordances. These proposed to work with all caps,
                which is no longer acceptable by the design system.
            </figcaption>
        </figure><figure class="mb-8">
            <Image src={cover} alt="" />
            <figcaption class="mt-2 mb-4 max-w-prose font-medium opacity-80">
                My implementation of the tabs aims to represent the active state
                better. Icons are made mandatory to support the button
                transparency, and the underline serves as an additional
                affordance. It took quite a lot of elbow grease to allow the
                component to look for error states in the form and navigate to
                the tab/step with the error. It is still an open ticket in our
                project to implement a way to scroll down to the field with the
                error after navigating to the tab/step with the error.
            </figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div><h3>Angular Material isn't customizable</h3><p>
        <a href="https://material.angular.io/">Angular Material</a> contains a decent
        set of components to build applications and a component development kit (CDK)
        to build custom components. However, the styling system based on SCSS variables
        is dated and implementing something like a dark mode switch, which is considered
        trivial in modern CSS with variables, <a
            href="https://github.com/angular/material.angular.io/tree/main/src/app/shared/theme-picker"
            >is not very straightforward</a
        >. This also affected the development of a <a
            href="/projects/20230608-customizing-colour-for-web-experiences"
            >colour customization</a
        > system for the <code>reseller-portal</code> and the <code
            >customer-portal</code
        >.
    </p><p>
        To be fair, the developers of Angular Material discourage <a
            href="https://material.angular.io/guide/customizing-component-styles#internal-component-elements"
            >overriding styles</a
        > but this makes it difficult to leave the door open for the library to be
        themed to fit the brand image in the future.
    </p><p>
        The <a
            href="https://angular.dev/roadmap#improve-angular-material-and-the-cdk"
            >Angular Roadmap</a
        > does mention the following, which is a change to look forward to.
    </p><blockquote>
        <p>
            To provide better customization of our Angular Material components
            and enable Material 3 capabilities, we'll be collaborating with
            Google's Material Design team on defining token-based theming APIs.
            As of Q4 2023, we're refactor.ing components to use the new API,
            finalizing the comprehensive set of tokens, and updating the Sass
            API based on the new tokens.
        </p>
    </blockquote><h3>Angular lacks headless libraries</h3><p>
        In a team small teams that I have worked in, getting things done right
        and getting things done fast are both high priority objectives. Both the
        Angular CDK and <a href="https://www.spartan.ng/">spartan.ng</a> can be thought
        of as headless UI libraries which can be used to build your own custom components.
        However, the functionality that they provide is quite basic when compared
        to projects like <a href="https://www.bits-ui.com/docs/introduction"
            >Bits UI</a
        > for Svelte, and <a href="https://www.radix-ui.com/">Radix UI</a> for React.
    </p><p>
        Alternatively, instead of Angular Material and the CDK, we could have
        use a larger component library like <a href="https://primeng.org/"
            >PrimeNG</a
        > or <a href="https://ng.ant.design/">NGZORRO</a>. However, both of them
        fall short in ways similar to Angular Material. They cannot be
        customized to fit a certain visual language and building custom
        components is not supported. PrimeNG does show promise with their <a
            href="https://primeng.org/theming">move toward better theming</a
        >.
    </p><h3>Tooling for Angular libraries is dated</h3><p>
        <a href="https://angular.io/guide/creating-libraries"
            >Angular libraries</a
        >
        <a href="https://github.com/ng-packagr/ng-packagr/issues/1471"
            >do not support PostCSS</a
        > which would prevent us from using <a href="https://tailwindcss.com/"
            >TailwindCSS</a
        >, which the team has grown very fond of. On top of this, using Angular
        libraries with npm workspaces turned out to be a bit of a struggle so we
        had to co-locate the project using <a
            href="https://nx.dev/angular-tutorial/1-code-generation">Nx</a
        > which provides adequate tooling to build an Angular library that can be
        reused while still using PostCSS, and as an extension, TailwindCSS. <strong
            >I'm not a critic of bundlers but having a build system felt like
            overkill for something like this.</strong
        >
    </p><h3>Storybook doesn't quite work</h3><p>
        I am very drawn to the paradigm of UI development that <a
            href="https://storybook.js.org/">Storybook</a
        > brings to the table. It also allows documentation to live closer to the
        developer which is one of the goals I wanted to achieve with this rewrite.
        However, to my disappointmen.t, it is <a
            href="https://github.com/storybookjs/storybook/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Aangular"
            >not very well supported in Angular</a
        >.
    </p><h3>Forms aren't truly declarative</h3><p>
        Angular is known for being a batteries included framework and it has
        been praised for having a declarative form library called <a
            href="https://angular.io/guide/reactive-forms">ReactiveForms</a
        > similar to <a href="https://formik.org/">Formik</a> inbuilt. Consider the
        following comparisons between form handling in React and Angular:
    </p><p>
        A simple <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/yup">Yup</a> or <a
            href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/zod">Zod</a
        > schema works with popular form handling libraries in the React ecosystem
        like Formik and react-hook-form. Setting up validation and error messages
        is extremely easy using Formik or <a
            href="https://react-hook-form.com/get-started#Applyvalidation"
            >react-hook-form</a
        >. Defining schemas in Angular's ReactiveForms is similar. Defining
        validation states and displaying messages is complicated. Conditional
        validation with <a
            href="https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/discussions/938">Zod</a
        > and <a
            href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/49394391/conditional-validation-in-yup"
            >Yup</a
        > is <em>relatively</em> straight-forward.
    </p>
    <p>
        Conditional validation with ReactiveForms requires a validator on the
        whole FormGroup, and a lot of imperative code. Due to <a
            href="https://angular.io/guide/change-detection-zone-pollution#resolving-zone-pollution"
            >how Angular does change detection</a
        >, this method also performs horribly and may cause the tab's memory to
        run out in complex configurations.
    </p><h2>Was it worth it?</h2>
    <figure>
        <Image src={img5} alt="" />
        <figcaption>
            We expect an increase in product awareness with the newer navigation
            bars. The information has better semantics and aims to showcase the
            product's capabilities better.
        </figcaption>
    </figure><figure>
        <Image src={img6} alt="" />
        <figcaption>
            The older navigation made less semantic sense when trying to explain
            hierarchies in the service.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <Image
            src={cover}
            class="w-full"
            alt='"Multiple tabs in a form with a warning symbol signifying an error on the tab."'
        />
        <figcaption>
            With adequate signalling of error states on tabs and inputs,
            previously unavailable, we expect better completion rates.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2">
            <Image
                src={img7}
                class="w-full h-full"
                alt='"A lighthouse performance score of 66 with a 2 second FCP, 8.7 second LCP, 200ms blocking time, and 0 CLS."'
            />
            <Image
                src={img8}
                alt='"A lighthouse performance score of 81 with a 1.6 second FCP, 1.6 second LCP, 370ms blocking time, and 0.016 CLS."'
            />
        </div>
        <figcaption>
            The reduction in bundle sizes and other optimizations contributed to
            a better Lighthouse performance score on the newer application but
            there remains a lot more to optimize.
        </figcaption>
    </figure><h2>What would I do differently today?</h2><p>
        Small startups like Cryptlex usually have quick iteration cycles and
        require a certain speed of work to achieve them. With this in mind, it
        is always great to have an extendable set of visually consistent
        components to start with.
    </p><p>
        All frameworks that I have used so far have their idiosyncrasies. In my
        time writing React, I can say that it is equally difficult to understand
        and work with the framework (read <a
            href="https://legacy.reactjs.org/docs/hooks-effect.html"
            >useEffect</a
        >) as it is with Angular (read <a
            href="https://angular.io/api/forms/ControlValueAccessor"
            >ControlValueAccessor</a
        >, <a href="https://angular.io/api/common/NgTemplateOutlet"
            >NgTemplateOutlet</a
        >).
    </p><p>
        However, React has a rich ecosystem of libraries like <a
            href="https://ui.shadcn.com/">shadcn/ui</a
        > and <a href="https://tanstack.com/table/latest">Tanstack Table</a> that
        make it easier to build UIs with it. If I was building today, I would probably
        pick React, or Svelte, despite their set of limitations and issues.
    </p>
</ProjectPost>
